### Formal Definition of Map Views & Map.Entry<K, V>

A Map does not implement `Iterable` directly, but provides three distinct collection views:
1. **`Set<K> keySet()`**: A view of all unique keys.
2. **`Collection<V> values()`**: A view of all values (duplicates permitted).
3. **`Set<Map.Entry<K, V>> entrySet()`**: A view of all key-value pairs as `Map.Entry` objects.

Each `Map.Entry<K, V>` exposes:
* `K getKey()`: The associated key.
* `V getValue()`: The associated value.
* `V setValue(V value)`: Replaces the value in the backing map.

---

### The Performance Anti-Pattern: keySet() vs entrySet()

```java
// Anti-Pattern: 2N operations!
for (String key : map.keySet()) {
    Integer val = map.get(key); // SECONDARY HASH LOOKUP ON EVERY ITERATION!
}

// Recommended: N operations!
for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : map.entrySet()) {
    String key = entry.getKey();
    Integer val = entry.getValue(); // Direct pointer access, zero hash calculation!
}
```
In a map of $1,000,000$ entries, `keySet() + map.get()` executes **$1,000,000$ unnecessary hash calculations and bucket traversals**. `entrySet()` traverses the backing node array once, running twice as fast.

---

### Modern Java 8+ Atomic Map Primitives

Modern Java introduces powerful functional operations on `Map`:
* **`computeIfAbsent(key, k -> new ArrayList<>())`**: Computes and inserts the value if absent, returning the existing or newly created value. Ideal for multi-maps!
* **`merge(key, 1, Integer::sum)`**: If `key` is absent, sets value to `1`. If present, applies `Integer::sum(oldVal, 1)`. Perfect for frequency counters!
* **`forEach((k, v) -> ...)`**: Concise lambda iteration.

```java
import java.util.*;

public class ModernMapIterationDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map<String, Integer> wordCounts = new HashMap<>();

        // 1. One-line frequency counting with merge()
        List<String> words = List.of("apple", "banana", "apple", "cherry", "banana", "apple");
        for (String w : words) {
            wordCounts.merge(w, 1, Integer::sum);
        }
        System.out.println("1. Word frequencies: " + wordCounts);

        // 2. High-speed iteration with entrySet()
        int totalWords = 0;
        for (Map.Entry<String, Integer> entry : wordCounts.entrySet()) {
            totalWords += entry.getValue();
        }
        System.out.println("2. Total words counted: " + totalWords);

        // 3. Multi-map grouping with computeIfAbsent()
        Map<String, List<String>> categoryMap = new HashMap<>();
        categoryMap.computeIfAbsent("fruit", k -> new ArrayList<>()).add("apple");
        categoryMap.computeIfAbsent("fruit", k -> new ArrayList<>()).add("banana");
        categoryMap.computeIfAbsent("grain", k -> new ArrayList<>()).add("rice");
        System.out.println("3. Multi-map grouping: " + categoryMap);
    }
}
```

**Console Output:**
```text
1. Word frequencies: {banana=2, cherry=1, apple=3}
2. Total words counted: 6
3. Multi-map grouping: {fruit=[apple, banana], grain=[rice]}
```

---

### Iteration Approaches Summary

| Approach | Read Overhead | Idiomatic For |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **`entrySet()` loop** | $O(N)$ (Optimal) | Reading or updating both Key & Value |
| **`map.forEach((k, v) -> ...)`** | $O(N)$ (Optimal) | Functional / Stream pipelines |
| **`keySet()` loop** | $O(N)$ keys only | When values are not needed |
| **`values()` loop** | $O(N)$ values only | Sums, averages, aggregations |
| **`keySet() + map.get()`** | $O(2N)$ (Double lookup) | **Anti-pattern: Avoid in production** |

